Divide longs by 32 bit divisors with 32 bit divisions.

The routines behind ldiv and lrem used the generic 64 bit division of the
runtime library, which takes about 300 instructions even for small operands.
A divisor that fits in 32 bits is the usual case in fixed-point arithmetic,
so divide the dividend in 32 bit steps with the hardware divide of the
processor instead: a divisor below 65536 needs three divisions of 16 bit
digits, any other 32 bit divisor the usual two digit estimate and
correction. Larger divisors still use the library.

The interpreter calls the same routines.

--- a/cldc/src/vm/share/interpreter/InterpreterRuntime.cpp
+++ b/cldc/src/vm/share/interpreter/InterpreterRuntime.cpp
@@ -145,14 +145,87 @@
     Synchronizer::signal_waiters(stack_lock);
